    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm still a little hung up on the \"randomness\" aspect. When I'm writing about the possibility of error or the fact that these results aren't 100% certain due to sampling noise, how do I frame that without sounding like my data is unreliable? I want to acknowledge the inherent uncertainty in the statistical model without undermining the strength of the correlation I found.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can frame the \"randomness\" aspect by using phrases such as \"statistical significance suggests that the observed correlation may be due to a genuine association, with some uncertainty due to sampling variability.\" Alternatively, you could say that \"the finding is unlikely to be due to chance, but the precision of our estimates is limited by the sample size and measurement errors,\" which acknowledges the uncertainty without implying that the data is unreliable. You can also use phrases like \"there is a statistically significant association\" rather than \"the correlation is statistically significant,\" to avoid implying that the data is 100% accurate.",
