      "role": "user",
      "content": "one more thing... i'm struggling with how to write about \"outliers\" or \"noise\" in the discussion section. sometimes it feels like these weird data points just pop up and mess with the whole pattern, almost like they aren't even following the same rules as the rest of the set. is there a professional way to describe those kinds of unexpected disturbances in the data?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can describe \"outliers\" or \"noise\" in a neutral and non-judgmental way. For example, you could say \"these data points deviate significantly from the norm, and their exclusion does not significantly affect the overall results\" or \"some individuals exhibited unusual patterns, which can be attributed to sampling error or anomalies in the data collection process.\" Alternatively, you could frame them as \"moderators\" or \"modulating factors\" that introduced variability in the data. For instance, \"the influence of outliers and sampling noise limited our ability to discern a clear trend, but the results remained robust after accounting for these factors.\"",
      "source": "target",
